and a cornerstone of being an effective politician, right?

264
00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:41.480
You have to go into the community,

265
00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.660
meet these people, meet them where they're at

266
00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:47.690
and try to develop some consensus around your vision.

267
00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:49.930
Like, you might think this is what I wanna do.

268
00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:51.230
They don't want it,

269
00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:53.740
how much are you willing to bend and compromise?

270
00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:56.150
How can you get them on board with what you wanna do?

271
00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:58.460
Like all of these are the skills that come into play

272
00:10:58.460 --> 00:10:59.820
every minute of your day.
Yeah.

273
00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:01.050
Now I would imagine.

274
00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:02.864
Yeah no, absolutely.

275
00:11:02.864 --> 00:11:04.630
And outreach can be tough,

276
00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:05.880
especially as a local elected,

277
00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:09.440
you're battling, you know the fact that,

278
00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.020
especially in my seat,

279
00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:13.770
there's this legacy of not engaging with folks.

280
00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:15.986
And so that means that, you know,

281
00:11:15.986 --> 00:11:19.580
the level of people who think to call their council member

282
00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.840
when they're having an issue is relatively low.

283
00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:24.248
And so I go to try to, you know turn that up.

284
00:11:24.248 --> 00:11:27.480
I want people, you know might be a little bit weird

285
00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.480
but I want people complaining to my office, right?

286
00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:33.170
Like that's how I'm gonna be effective in my role

287
00:11:34.128 --> 00:11:35.870
but it can be tough right.

288
00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:37.933
To get that level of input.

289
00:11:39.443 --> 00:11:41.870
I think we've done a pretty good job so far.

290
00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:43.090
And you're absolutely right

291
00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:45.730
sometimes people aren't quite there with you, right.

292
00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:47.984
They're not seeing what you're seeing

293
00:11:47.984 --> 00:11:52.984
and that does present its own kind of like question, right.

294
00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:56.352
You can't leave your constituents behind

295
00:11:56.352 --> 00:11:59.440
it's just not, it's not the right thing to do

296
00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:02.663
but you also can't just say,

297
00:12:03.787 --> 00:12:06.097
"Do the wrong thing, make the wrong decision

298
00:12:06.097 --> 00:12:07.397
"because you think it will be unpopular

299
00:12:07.397 --> 00:12:08.857
"with your constituents."

300
00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:12.110
And that's where you kind of create

301
00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:14.650
that that where you have that tension, right?

302
00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:16.800
Yeah well globally,

303
00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.380
or kind of at a macro context

304
00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:21.392
people are so, you know, kind of,

305
00:12:21.392 --> 00:12:24.060
you know, disabused of any idea

306
00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:26.260
that their politicians have their interest at heart,

307
00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:27.550
you know, they're bought and paid for

308
00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:29.940
by special interest groups

309
00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:32.220
and they're just looking to get reelected

310
00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:34.580
and they're gonna pander to their base.

311
00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:37.350
And anything else is just a mirror distraction

312
00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:39.340
beyond fundraising, right.

313
00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:42.830
But you're a guy who kind of was foisted

314
00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:46.080
into the public eye by dent of the activist work

315
00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.103
that you did in particular that one kind

316
00:12:49.103 --> 00:12:51.262
of viral photograph of you with your hands up

317
00:12:51.262 --> 00:12:53.140
and the rifle in your face

318
00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:55.430
from the cops that was.
Shamar.

319
00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:59.303
Shamar right, sorry in like 2015, right.

320
00:12:59.303 --> 00:13:00.136
Yep.

321
00:13:00.136 --> 00:13:02.510
So this kind of, you know,

322
00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:05.870
becomes not really a calling card,

323
00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.280
but it becomes kind of emblazoned on people's minds

324
00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.668
that you're a man of the people, right?

325
00:13:09.668 --> 00:13:10.501
Yeah.

326
00:13:10.501 --> 00:13:13.370
But then as you become an elected official

327
00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:16.540
there's a blurring line between activism

328
00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:20.190
and kind of effecting responsible political change.

329
00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.330
Right, like I'm interested in like how that works.

330
00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:24.100
Like where does the activist end,

331
00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:26.516
and where does the elected official begin

332
00:13:26.516 --> 00:13:28.830
to those things kind of merged together?

333
00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:32.709
Or do you have to think of them as like separate identities?

334
00:13:32.709 --> 00:13:35.560
I think you have to be honest with yourself

335
00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:40.502
about what this job can do to people

336
00:13:40.502 --> 00:13:42.850
even if they have good intentions, right.

337
00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:46.300
And if you're honest about which to be clear

338
00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:49.070
I think usually when, from my experience

339
00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:52.100
when folks are maybe being called like a sellout,

340
00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.770
right like that term

341
00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:57.060
or that process of going from being,

342
00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:59.130
you know, okay I came in with all these ideals

343
00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:00.978
and you know maybe I'm a little bit,

344
00:14:00.978 --> 00:14:03.130
I've moved into this other category

345
00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:05.653
where people aren't really feeling me.

346
00:14:05.653 --> 00:14:06.934
I think that that happens

347
00:14:06.934 --> 00:14:09.240
because of exhaustion and it happens to people

348
00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.840
because they think it can't happen to them, you know?

349
00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:15.773
And so for me, it's about keeping perspective

350
00:14:15.773 --> 00:14:20.620
and not taking some of that,

351
00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:23.181
some of that anger that people might have towards me,

352
00:14:23.181 --> 00:14:25.580
constituents or activists or whoever.

353
00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.920
Not taking it personal, because I know that

354
00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:30.230
there have been people, whether in my immediate seat

355
00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:33.260
or just in elected office before

356
00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:37.770
who have earned a lot of the bad will

357
00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:39.360
that people have for politicians,

358
00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:40.223
right?
Yeah.

359
00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.119
So you have to double down on the transparency

360
00:14:43.119 --> 00:14:45.640
and the outreach and the boots on the ground,

361
00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:46.610
in the community.

362
00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.790
And I think that you also have to,

363
00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:53.790
in a way, figure out how to be vulnerable,

364
00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:57.520
how, you know, it's not a quality

365
00:14:57.520 --> 00:14:59.280
that's rewarded in this position.

366
00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.700
Right, and there are ways to be sort of fully vulnerable

367
00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.730
right like I don't know, crying in public.

368
00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:05.910
Right.
Right.

369
00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:06.743
And there are ways.

370
00:15:06.743 --> 00:15:11.147
Performative on social media, yeah oversharing.

371
00:15:11.147 --> 00:15:12.650
Yeah absolutely.

372
00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:16.100
And then there are ways where to be vulnerable

373
00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.230
that can earn some good trust and that are real

374
00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:20.480
may not look vulnerable on their face, right.

375
00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:23.710
You know, there have been times

376
00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:27.970
where I have thought something was needed in my community.

377
00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.780
Like a couple of months ago I was advocating

378
00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:32.550
for a women's homeless shelter

379
00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:33.787
and the neighbors were like,

380
00:15:33.787 --> 00:15:34.817
"Council member,

381
00:15:34.817 --> 00:15:37.790
"hell no we don't want a homeless shelter here."

382
00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:42.007
And you know ultimately, you know I was,

383
00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:47.623
I it didn't prevail as much as I wanted it to.

384
00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:50.850
I had public meetings where I was just,

385
00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:53.523
Oh my God, I was just blasted at these meetings.

386
00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.340
Not in my backyard kind of thing.

387
00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:59.340
Yeah, and people with some legitimate fear

388
00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:00.850
right, like I think people have been fed

389
00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:05.850
a lot of gross narratives about homeless people

390
00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:11.910
and it's not my job to judge them

391
00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:14.370
for having consumed those messages

392
00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:16.860
but it is my job to maybe do some political education

393
00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.504
and try to help them unlearn some of those messages.

394
00:16:19.504 --> 00:16:21.220
And so when I'm looking at somebody

395
00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:25.240
who's using criminalizing language for homeless people,

396
00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:26.570
I disagree with them.

397
00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:31.140
I gotta work to make sure that I'm winning and not them,

398
00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:34.000
Yeah.
But I don't judge them,

399
00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.100
right, I don't dismiss them

400
00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:39.460
because I know in some ways like what they want

401
00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:42.260
and what I want and what that person who's living

402
00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:45.022
out on the street wants is probably pretty similar.

403
00:16:45.022 --> 00:16:47.313
They've just got it, they walked into the conversation

404
00:16:47.313 --> 00:16:49.640
with a lot of preconceived notions.

405
00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:51.950
Now, some of the people who were standing against me

406
00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.390
in that meeting, I thought you know

407
00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:57.640
I've lost this person support, right.

408
00:16:57.640 --> 00:16:59.490
Not only did I lose this person support,

409
00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:04.490
I also you know, some of the other electeds

410
00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:06.650
who were in the room where looked around

411
00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:07.727
saw me getting yelled at and thought,

412
00:17:07.727 --> 00:17:08.747
"Oh, I'm not gonna support it

413
00:17:08.747 --> 00:17:10.789
"'cause I don't wanna be in his position."

414
00:17:10.789 --> 00:17:12.320
Yeah.
And then we end up loosing.

415
00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:14.247
That shelter didn't get bill.

416
00:17:15.110 --> 00:17:17.171
And so, and so it's kind of a setback

417
00:17:17.171 --> 00:17:20.870
but you've got folks who stood against it at the time

418
00:17:20.870 --> 00:17:22.357
who have reached back out to say,

419
00:17:22.357 --> 00:17:24.590
"You know actually I see what you were doing there."

420
00:17:24.590 --> 00:17:26.444
You have folks who are in the back of the crowd,

421
00:17:26.444 --> 00:17:29.390
maybe seeing me get heckled.

422
00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:31.340
And you know, they're just everyday people.

423
00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:32.800
They don't wanna be heckled themselves

424
00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:33.960
but they agreed with me.

425
00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.500
And those are the kinds of things

426
00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:38.358
that can happen when you're willing to,

427
00:17:38.358 --> 00:17:42.120
you know go out on a limb when you're not operating

428
00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.670
in that mode of, "What's gonna get me liked in this moment."

429
00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:48.972
Right so you're your district is ward five

430
00:17:48.972 --> 00:17:52.559
it's 82% people of color yeah.

431
00:17:52.559 --> 00:17:55.520
And something like 40% of the population

432
00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:57.540
is below the poverty line.
Yeah.

433
00:17:57.540 --> 00:17:59.160
So this is the, you know,

434
00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:01.325
kind of modus from which you're operating

435
00:18:01.325 --> 00:18:05.943
and the people on behalf of whom you're advocating for.

436
00:18:05.943 --> 00:18:08.140
It's interesting that even then,

437
00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:09.438
there's blind spots like

438
00:18:09.438 --> 00:18:11.666
you're trying to champion the underdog

439
00:18:11.666 --> 00:18:13.737
but these other underdogs are like,

440
00:18:13.737 --> 00:18:14.828
"Hey, what about me?"

441
00:18:14.828 --> 00:18:15.661
Yeah.

442
00:18:15.661 --> 00:18:16.494
Like, it just gets complicated.

443
00:18:16.494 --> 00:18:17.530
It does get complicated.

444
00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:20.530
I think that's why, you know

445
00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:21.510
I think that's why that,

446
00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:24.040
like the only term I can think of is political Ed,

447
00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:27.430
like political education is just really important.

448
00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:29.650
It can't happen if you're dismissing people,

449
00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:32.499
it can't happen if you're condescending people,

450
00:18:32.499 --> 00:18:34.230
people are pretty smart,

451
00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:37.630
but they are also a lot of things on top of that.

452
00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:39.630
Right people might be afraid at any given moment.

453
00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:43.620
People might have not wrestled with their biases.

454
00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.549
Right, all these things can occur.

455
00:18:45.549 --> 00:18:49.340
And so, you know I think that's how,

456
00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:51.010
you can find yourself in a position like that.

457
00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:53.340
And I know for some of those folks,

458
00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:54.730
they thought, they're thinking,

459
00:18:54.730 --> 00:18:57.622
well, yeah I'm poor right.

460
00:18:57.622 --> 00:19:01.188
And this guy, you know meaning me,

461
00:19:01.188 --> 00:19:06.188
wants to "dump more poor people in my neighborhood"

462
00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:08.610
And eliminate amount of resources

463
00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:10.720
into a program that doesn't benefit my life.

464
00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:12.130
Right.
Yeah.

465
00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:13.580
And you know, it's a scarcity mindset.

466
00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:16.021
You know, my mindset is, you know

467
00:19:16.021 --> 00:19:19.340
you're greeted when you arrive

468
00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:21.557
you're given what you're needed, right.

469
00:19:21.557 --> 00:19:25.190
And I'm always gonna have that attitude.

470
00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:27.590
And so I'll continue to make sure,

471
00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:29.580
you know make sure that we're doing our fair share

472
00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:31.283
in building shelters,

473
00:19:31.283 --> 00:19:34.703
but you know, I can I can disagree

474
00:19:34.703 --> 00:19:38.920
while empathizing with someone who might be advocating

475
00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:40.990
for a position that I can't stand on.

476
00:19:40.990 --> 00:19:43.140
Yeah well, that's your job right.

477
00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:44.453
You're constantly dealing with people

478
00:19:44.453 --> 00:19:47.464
that don't see things that you do, right.

479
00:19:47.464 --> 00:19:50.370
I mean, you're like in the shit right now

480
00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:51.940
like you're in the cross hairs

481
00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:54.860
the entire world is paying attention

482
00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.540
to what's happening in Minneapolis right now.

483
00:19:57.540 --> 00:19:59.730
I would imagine that that must feel

484
00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:03.080
at times burdensome to carry that kind of level

485
00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:06.080
of responsibility and you know,

486
00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.046
on the subject of political education

487
00:20:09.046 --> 00:20:10.980
and boots on the ground.

488
00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:13.860
I mean, that's my motivation in coming here.

489
00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:16.240
Like everybody else I've been watching

490
00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:18.590
what's been going on here over the last year.

491
00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:22.330
This feels like a very important historic moment.

492
00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:25.290
We're at a sort of crossroads,

493
00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:27.910
I think with what's going on in the city

494
00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:30.790
and there's what's gonna happen with the verdict

495
00:20:30.790 --> 00:20:35.260
and how is Minneapolis gonna move forward or not from this.

496
00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:38.930
But I think also because everybody's paying

497
00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:39.920
such close attention

498
00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:42.886
the ripple effect in terms of like how we're gonna function

499
00:20:42.886 --> 00:20:45.460
as a nation and even across the world,

500
00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:47.890
I think has profound implications, right.

501
00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:50.788
And I just wanted to share with you yesterday,

502
00:20:50.788 --> 00:20:53.500
you know as somebody who thought

503
00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.390
they were paying fairly close attention

504
00:20:55.390 --> 00:20:57.883
to what was happening here, I haven't been here before.

505
00:20:57.883 --> 00:21:00.743
I mean, the last time I visited Minneapolis I was a kid

506
00:21:00.743 --> 00:21:04.667
and we went to George Floyd square yesterday.

507
00:21:04.667 --> 00:21:08.760
And in my mind as like this progressive,

508
00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:13.760
you know conscious citizen, I thought I knew what to expect.

509
00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:17.160
And it defied all of that.

510
00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:18.810
Like all of that went out the window

511
00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:20.040
as soon as I arrived there.

512
00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:22.180
And it was nothing like what I thought it would be

513
00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:24.340
from the setting to the neighborhood

514
00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:26.270
to the visceral experience

515
00:21:26.270 --> 00:21:28.870
of being in what is really a living, breathing

516
00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:33.239
not just Memorial, but museum grieving place,

517
00:21:33.239 --> 00:21:34.860
gathering spot.

518
00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:36.629
Like it's so many things you can't define it

519
00:21:36.629 --> 00:21:37.860
as any one thing.

520
00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:41.345
And I was not expecting to be as moved as I was by it

521
00:21:41.345 --> 00:21:43.920
or as welcomed as I was.

522
00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:47.880
We had a great encounter with the Agape folks.

523
00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:49.690
You know, that group

524
00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:52.128
who then, we'd spent like an hour there

525
00:21:52.128 --> 00:21:52.961
and then we were getting ready to leave.

526
00:21:52.961 --> 00:21:54.120
